  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure has pushed into the region, but low level moisture\ntrapped under a subsidence inversion has kept extensive cloud\ncover across the region today. This moist layer will start to\nscour out this afternoon with some clearing this evening and\novernight, however clouds will likely increase again tomorrow\nmorning.\n\nHigh pressure will center itself over the region tomorrow, with\ndry conditions. However, upper level clouds will increase and\nstill likely going to see mostly cloudy conditions through the\nday. Warm air advection on southwesterly flow will push \ntemperatures into the 70s with upper 60s in the Catskills.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nWarm front will cross the region tomorrow night through Thursday\nmorning. This will bring a few light showers across the area,\nbut nothing substantial. The next chance for rain will come on\nFriday, when the cold front crosses the region. Scattered showers\nare expected across the area on Friday, and QPF is currently at\na quarter inch or less across most of the region. The front \nexits the region Friday night with Canadian high pressure \npushing in behind it. Temperatures will be 10 to 15 degrees \ncooler on Saturday than Thursday and Friday's highs that top out\nin the mid to upper 70s. Overnight lows could get into the low \nto mid 30s Saturday night across northern Oneida County, but \nthere remains some uncertainty with cloud cover, as another \nsystem approaches by early Sunday morning. \n\nAt this time, there are some timing differences amongst the\nmodel guidance on an upper trough/cold front dropping out of\nCanada Sunday into Monday and low pressure tracking off the Mid\nAtlantic Coast to our south. A faster and stronger cold front\nwill keep the track of the low to far south to bring substantial\nmoisture into region. This would make shower activity on Sunday\nfairly light with scattered coverage. For now, the NBM has about\n30 to 40 percent shower chances from the Twin Tiers on southward\nand 20 to 30 percent chances across Central NY. Due to the\nuncertainty, decided not to stray from the NBM solution at this\ntime.",
